## Project context
- Node.js/TypeScript (ESM) service for vehicle transactions
- Strict Clean Architecture with layered separation and ports/adapters
- Stack: Fastify + @ts-rest, MongoDB, RabbitMQ, Winston, Zod, Vitest
- Configuration handled via `EnvironmentConfigLoader`
- Resource lifecycle via `ManagedResource` pattern (initialize/dispose)
- Tooling: pnpm, ESLint (@zcorp), Prettier, Vitest, Docker

### Structure and dependencies (Clean Architecture)
- `src/domain`: Entities, domain errors, repository interfaces
- `src/application`: Use cases, services, ports (interfaces), orchestration of business logic
- `src/infrastructure`: Adapters (port implementations), repositories, external integrations, resources
- `src/presentation`: HTTP API (Fastify + @ts-rest), controllers, applications (main/cli)

Dependency rules (must hold for any new code):
- `presentation` → depends on `application` (and shared types) ONLY, NEVER on `infrastructure`
- `application` → depends on `domain` (and shared types) ONLY, NEVER on `infrastructure`
- `domain` → depends on nothing (shared types allowed)
- `infrastructure` → depends on `application` (ports) and `domain`
- Dependency composition happens in `presentation/applications/*.application.ts`

## Code conventions
- TypeScript ESM only. Local imports must use `.js` file extension (e.g., `../file.js`)
- Prefer named exports; avoid `default export`
- No I/O or network access within use cases (pure logic). Use ports for external interactions
- Constructor injection for all dependencies (ports, services, logger)
- Respect schemas/types from `@zcorp/shared-typing-wheelz` and `@zcorp/wheelz-contracts`. Do not re-define types
- Domain-specific errors live in `src/domain/errors` and must be propagated up to presentation where they are mapped to HTTP
- Logging via `LoggerPort` (Winston). Do not use `console.log`
- Never leak infrastructure adapters into `application` or `domain`
- Files and naming:
  - Classes: PascalCase, Ports suffixed with `Port`, Adapters named by technology
  - Use cases suffixed `.use-case.ts`, Services suffixed `.service.ts`

### Ports & Adapters
- Define every external dependency as a `Port` (interface) under `src/application/ports`
- Implement the port under `src/infrastructure/adapters/<tech>/<name>.<adapter>.ts`
- Adapters contain no business logic; only mapping, I/O/network calls, and error handling

### Repositories
- Interfaces under `src/domain/repositories`
- Implementations under `src/infrastructure/repositories`
- No direct database access from `application`/`presentation`

### Configuration
- Read configuration using `EnvironmentConfigLoader` and `configSchema`
- Validate any new environment variable in the Zod schema

### API (Fastify + @ts-rest)
- Define routes using `@zcorp/wheelz-contracts`
- Map domain errors → HTTP status in routers (e.g., 422 for `InvalidTransactionError`, 404 for `TransactionNotFoundError`)
- Protect routes with `authPlugin`, `requireAuth`, `requireAllRoles`, etc., consistent with existing usage

### Queue / MQ
- Use `QueuePort` for all MQ interactions
- Implementations handle resilience and ack/nack (see `ResilientRabbitMQQueue`)

### Logging
- Use `LoggerPort` everywhere; messages must be clear and contextualized

## Language and localization
- All user-facing messages (HTTP, CLI), logs, comments, and tests MUST be written in English.
- When touching existing French strings, prefer migrating them to English in the same edit, unless contract compatibility forbids it.

## Tests (Vitest) – Arrange, Act, Assert REQUIRED
- Framework: Vitest; prefer mocks from `vitest-mock-extended`
- Test files: `*.spec.ts`
- Scope: unit tests for use cases, services, controllers, and mappings; no real I/O
- Mock all ports/adapters/network. No external calls in unit tests
- Strict AAA per test:
  - Arrange: prepare data, mocks, SUT
  - Act: execute the method under test
  - Assert: verify state and interactions

Example (AAA) – use case test:
```ts
import { describe, it, expect, vi } from 'vitest';
import { mockDeep } from 'vitest-mock-extended';
import { CreateVehicleTransactionUseCase } from '@/application/use-cases/create-vehicle-transaction.use-case.js';
import type { DataSignerPort } from '@/application/ports/data-signer.port.js';
import type { DateProviderPort } from '@/application/ports/date-provider.port.js';
import type { IdGeneratorPort } from '@/application/ports/id-generator.port.js';
import type { QueuePort } from '@/application/ports/queue.port.js';
import type { TransactionRepository } from '@/domain/repositories/transaction.repository.js';

describe('CreateVehicleTransactionUseCase', () => {
  it('creates and enqueues a transaction', async () => {
    // Arrange
    const dataSigner = mockDeep<DataSignerPort>();
    const dateProvider: DateProviderPort = { now: () => new Date('2024-01-01T00:00:00Z') };
    const idGenerator: IdGeneratorPort = { generate: vi.fn().mockResolvedValue('tx-1') };
    const repository = mockDeep<TransactionRepository>();
    const queue = mockDeep<QueuePort>();

    dataSigner.sign.mockResolvedValue({ signAlgorithm: 'RSA-SHA256', signature: 'sig' });

    const sut = new CreateVehicleTransactionUseCase(
      dataSigner,
      dateProvider,
      idGenerator,
      repository,
      queue
    );

    const vehicle = { vin: 'VF1AAAAA', features: {} as any, infos: {} as any, history: [], technicalControls: [], attachedClientsIds: [], sinisterInfos: {} as any };

    // Act
    const result = await sut.execute({ action: 'create', data: vehicle }, 'user-1');

    // Assert
    expect(result.id).toBe('tx-1');
    expect(repository.save).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.objectContaining({ id: 'tx-1' }));
    expect(queue.enqueue).toHaveBeenCalledOnce();
  });
});
```

Additional test guidance:
- Test domain errors (e.g., `InvalidTransactionError`) and HTTP conversion in routers
- For controllers/services: verify orchestration (ports called with correct parameters)
- Avoid snapshot tests for business logic; prefer explicit assertions

## Quality, Lint & Formatting
- Use `pnpm run lint` (ESLint with `@zcorp` config + local rules)
- Fix all errors and warnings; avoid unjustified overrides
- `pnpm run build` must stay green; do not break APIs or types

## Common scripts
- Dev API: `pnpm run dev` (see Dockerfile target `development`)
- Lint: `pnpm run lint`
- Build: `pnpm run build`
- Test: `pnpm run test`

## CI/CD
- PRs must pass lint, build, test (see `.github/workflows/lint-test.yml`)
- On `main`, build & push Docker image then trigger infra deployment

## Implementation playbooks
### Add a new Use Case
1) Create the class under `src/application/use-cases/xxx.use-case.ts` (pure logic)
2) Dependencies via ports (interfaces) – injected via constructor
3) Test with AAA using port mocks
4) Expose via `TransactionService` if relevant
5) Router/Controller only if public HTTP exposure is needed

### Add a new Port + Adapter
1) Define the interface under `src/application/ports`
2) Implement the adapter under `src/infrastructure/adapters/<tech>/<name>....ts`
3) Register the adapter in the corresponding `Application` (main/cli)
4) Test the adapter with isolated integration tests (optional) or underlying client mocks

### Add an HTTP route
1) Extend `transactionContract` on the contracts side (external repo if centralized)
2) Implement handler in `TransactionRouter`, mapping errors → statuses
3) Add auth/roles hooks as needed
4) Cover controller + router (success/errors) with Vitest

## Security & Secrets
- Never hardcode secrets; use `.env` and `configSchema`
- Do not log sensitive data